# NOT mdg-demo. Every directory above is a genuinely independent repository with
# its own .git, cloned alongside this workspace. mdg-demo is not — it has no
# .git and 153 of its files are tracked right here. Listing it as ignored was
# therefore half-true in the worst possible way: git keeps honouring the files
# already tracked, and silently swallows every NEW one. That is how the entire
# marketing film (src/marketing/*, 8 files), the stock-variation video's own
# component, and two build scripts came to exist on one laptop and nowhere else.
# mdg-demo/.gitignore already excludes the things that genuinely should not be
# committed — node_modules, out/, the generated voiceovers, .env — so the blanket
# rule bought nothing and cost source code.

# graphify knowledge graph (generated).
# graph.json is ~7 MB and graph.html ~5 MB, and the post-commit hook rewrites
# them on every commit, so committing them would bloat history fast. Rebuild
# locally instead: graphify extract . --no-gitignore
# (If this ever becomes a team workspace, graphify's docs recommend committing
# graphify-out/ so everyone starts with a map — drop this block then.)
graphify-out/

# Misc
*.tgz
.pids

# Secrets that live in the working tree so tooling can reach them.
#
# The AWS key for the backend box sits in .secrets/ because macOS refuses this
# machine's automation read access to ~/Downloads, and every deploy needs it.
# A private key one `git add -A` away from a remote is how credentials leak, so
# the ignore rule goes in BEFORE the key does. Never remove it.
.secrets/
*.pem
*.p12

# Working reports and one-off pages written at the repo root — a performance
# write-up, a plan, a runbook. They are notes to ourselves, they go stale the
# week they are written, and several describe how the portal automation works
# in detail. `one-login-many-services.html` reached a commit before this rule
# existed; it is untracked here in the same change.
/*.html
